============================================================================================
PART 1.  Quantum-walk speedups are measured against a RANDOM WALK.
         The method actually used is DESCENT. All three, same landscape.
============================================================================================
  sqrt(RW) is the BEST a quantum walk can ever do (Szegedy quadratic).

  problem             n         RW     descent   sqrt(RW) QW wins?
  ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Heis N=4 b0         4       16.3         2.0        4.0       no
  Heis N=4 b1         4       16.0         1.9        4.0       no
  Heis N=6 b0         6       75.0         3.1        8.7       no
  Heis N=6 b1         6       75.8         3.1        8.7       no

  RW ~ 2^n, so the quantum ceiling is 2^(n/2) - still EXPONENTIAL.
  Descent is ~n/2, POLYNOMIAL. Improving the exponent of an
  exponential loses to something that was never exponential.

============================================================================================
PART 2.  THE DECODE-SCALING CONJECTURE, TESTED BELOW 1 SHOT/VERTEX
============================================================================================
  The notes predict the LINEAR walk decode overtakes the NONLINEAR
  Boltzmann decode once spv < 1. v52 stopped at n=8 (spv=32).
  Landscape: degree<=2 Walsh, which T8 says is the real shape.

     n      2^n       spv    walk cos   boltz cos   winner
  --------------------------------------------------------
     8      256     32.00      0.6904      0.8337    boltz
    10     1024      8.00      0.6864      0.8069    boltz
    12     4096      2.00      0.6442      0.7531    boltz
    14    16384      0.50      0.5845      0.7469    boltz
    15    32768      0.25      0.6048      0.7081    boltz

  REFUTED. Boltzmann wins at every size including spv=0.25. The
  "needs shots >~ 2^n" reasoning has a hole: you do not need every
  vertex, only enough good ones - 8192 shots still reach ~7000
  distinct vertices out of 32768. The walk is capped by MODEL
  MISSPECIFICATION (degree-1 only), not by shot noise.

============================================================================================
PART 3.  BARREN PLATEAU - does the walk signal decay more slowly?
============================================================================================
  Deep random ansatz (reps = 2N). grad_var is the BP signature.
  walk_sig is the degree-1 Walsh coefficient the drift rides on.

     N   reps      grad_var    walk_sig^2       ratio
  ----------------------------------------------------
     4      8     3.243e-02     1.034e-02      0.3188
     6     12     7.764e-03     2.475e-03      0.3188
     8     16     2.292e-03     7.308e-04      0.3188

  gradient  decays as 2^(-0.956 N)
  walk sig  decays as 2^(-0.956 N)
  ratio is sin^2(R) = sin^2(0.6) = 0.318821  EXACTLY, at every N.

  The walk drift IS sin(R) times the gradient - a CONSTANT multiple.
  A constant multiple of an exponentially small quantity is
  exponentially small at the SAME rate. This is v89 confirmed, and
  it is arithmetic, not a theorem about quantum walks in general.

============================================================================================
PART 4.  THE MECHANISM IS REAL - thin tall barrier (spike potential)
============================================================================================
  Classical annealing goes OVER: cost ~ exp(height).
  Quantum tunnels THROUGH: cost ~ exp(width), independent of height.

  height   width    class. steps    class. succ   quantum P(0)
  --------------------------------------------------------------
     2.0     1.0             647           1.00      7.340e-03
     5.0     1.0            1158           1.00      4.348e-03
    10.0     1.0           60711           0.90      7.163e-03
    20.0     1.0          200000           0.00      2.733e-03

  Classical success collapses 1.00 -> 0.00 while quantum transmission
  stays flat. THE TUNNELING ADVANTAGE IS REAL AND CONSTRUCTIBLE.
  CAVEAT: quantum T is maximised over a small grid, and P(0) ~ 0.5%
  means ~200 repetitions per success - constant in height, not free.

  So the open question is NOT a better mixer. It is whether an ansatz
  can be designed whose landscape has tall THIN barriers. Measured
  width on efficient_su2(reps=1) Heisenberg N=4/N=6 was 0.96 / 0.99
  of the path - WIDE, so the mechanism does not engage there.

============================================================================================
PART 5.  k_steps COLLAPSE - the inner loop is one gate, exactly
============================================================================================
  Every step applies a controlled single-qubit rotation per param
  qubit, all on the SAME ancilla. C-A . C-B = C-(BA), so the whole
  loop is ONE controlled-SU(2) per qubit, found by classical 2x2
  multiplication before the circuit is built.

   k_steps ||U_loop-U_1||   depth loop    depth new    ratio
  ------------------------------------------------------------
         2      3.679e-15           12            5     2.4x
         4      2.942e-15           24            5     4.8x
         8      3.858e-15           48            5     9.6x
        16      4.858e-15           96            5    19.2x
        64      3.517e-15          384            5    76.8x

  Exact to machine precision at every k. On the FULL walk circuit
  this is 3.5x depth and 3x CX at the shipped k_steps=15, with an
  IDENTICAL unitary. It also makes k_steps free, since the cost no
  longer depends on it.

  NOTE: this applies to the PRODUCT mixer only. It works because
  sum_i (a_i Z_i + b X_i) is separable. A global reflection is
  rank-1 and does NOT collapse.

